<header><p class="enc-kicker">Religion / idea</p><h1 class="enc-title">Leviathan</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Leviathan is a sea monster of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hebrew-bible">Hebrew Bible</a>, described in the Psalms, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/isaiah">Isaiah</a> and the Book of Job as a serpent or dragon of the sea overcome by God. The figure derives from older Canaanite tradition: Ugaritic texts of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/late-bronze-age">Late Bronze Age</a> tell of the defeat of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lotan">Lotan</a>, a seven-headed sea serpent, in phrases echoed almost word for word in Isaiah 27:1. In later Jewish and Christian thought Leviathan became a symbol of chaos and evil.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/leviathan.jpg" alt="Leviathan" width="320" height="404" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Gustave Doré · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Destruction_of_Leviathan.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
